COMPUTATION MODEL
----------------------------------------------------------------------
A cellular automaton is best understood as a parallel, local-transition 
computation model whose global behavior mirrors the classical automata 
hierarchy all the way up to Turing universality. The write‑up you 
provided is already strong; below I’ll refine it into a more 
structurally explicit, operator‑clean, and automata‑theoretic synthesis 
that matches your analytical style.

CORE STRUCTURAL TAKEAWAY
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A CA is a finite‑alphabet, shift‑invariant, synchronous transition 
operator

    G : Σ^ℤ → Σ^ℤ

generated by a local rule

    δ : Σ^k → Σ,

where k is the neighborhood size. The global operator is the parallel 
extension

    G(w)_i = δ(w_{i−r}, …, w_i, …, w_{i+r}),

exactly the same way the extended transition of a DFA applies to 
strings, except performed in parallel across all sites.

This parallelism is the key structural difference: CAs are local 
automata with global synchronous composition.

AUTOMATA‑THEORETIC PLACEMENT
----------------------------------------------------------------------

1. FINITE AUTOMATA / REGULAR LANGUAGES
--------------------------------------
A CA on a ring of size n has a global state space Σ^n. Thus it *is* a 
DFA with:
    - state set Q = Σ^n,
    - transition function G,
    - no input tape.

Restricted families of CAs generate regular languages, and Myhill–
Nerode applies to the set of distinguishable local neighborhoods.

Key object: the local context equivalence relation

    u ≡_δ v  ⇔  δ(context(u)) = δ(context(v)).


2. PUSHDOWN AUTOMATA / CONTEXT‑FREE LANGUAGES
----------------------------------------------
Increasing neighborhood radius or adding multi‑component cell states 
yields effective stack‑like behavior. These CAs can emulate context‑
free languages but are not limited to them.


3. TURING MACHINES
------------------
A CA with sufficiently rich local rules is Turing‑universal. Rule 110 
is the canonical example.

A TM configuration (q, i, w) is encoded as a CA configuration with:
    - tape contents in the cell states,
    - head position encoded by a special marker,
    - TM state encoded locally.

Thus:

    CA universality ⇒ computable = recursive = r.e.

This places CAs at the same level as Turing machines, λ‑calculus, and 
μ‑recursive functions.

LANGUAGES ASSOCIATED WITH A CA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

For a CA G, one studies languages such as:

• Quiescent‑reachability language
      L_halt = { w : ∃t  G^t(w) = ε^∞ }.
  This is undecidable by reduction from the classical halting 
  problem.

• Orbit language
      L_orbit(w₀) = { G^t(w₀) : t ≥ 0 }.

• Space‑time diagram languages
  Accepted by local predicates on the 2D diagram.

These are typically recursively enumerable, and many are non‑recursive.

Mapping reductions and Turing reductions transfer directly.

COMPLEXITY THEORY INSIDE CA DYNAMICS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Restrict to finite grids of size n. Then:

Prediction problem:
    “What is the state of cell i after t steps?”
Complexity depends on:
    - the rule,
    - encoding of t (unary vs binary).

This problem can lie in:
    P, NP, PSPACE, EXP.

Many structural CA problems are PSPACE‑complete:
    - reachability,
    - Garden‑of‑Eden detection,
    - reversibility.

Oracle layers allow simulation of oracle machines, and polynomial‑time 
reductions remain the standard completeness tool.

CLOSURE PROPERTIES AND PUMPING
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Restricted CAs generate regular languages, so:
    union, intersection, complement,
    concatenation, Kleene star

all hold, and the classical pumping lemma applies.

For richer CA families, one obtains:
    - context‑free pumping analogues,
    - higher‑order iteration lemmas.

STRUCTURAL SUMMARY IN OPERATOR FORM
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A CA is a parallel automaton with:
    - instantaneous descriptions w ∈ Σ^ℤ,
    - local transition δ,
    - global operator G = δ^parallel,
    - evolution G^t(w).

When δ is universal:
    - CA = TM in computational power,
    - recursive / r.e. languages appear,
    - halting is undecidable,
    - complexity classes P, NP, PSPACE, EXP arise naturally.

Thus the full automata‑theoretic toolkit—regular languages, CFGs, PDAs,
Myhill–Nerode, pumping, reductions—extends cleanly to CA dynamics.

